    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Kop',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Kop (also known as Cop) is a traditional Polish trick-taking game that is a cultural staple of the Greater Poland (Wielkopolska) region. Played with a highly condensed 16-card deck, it is celebrated for its extreme speed and complex partnership dynamics. It is a descendant of the German Schafkopf and is the sister game to the Kashubian BaÅ›ka.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Developed from the German Schafkopf game in the 19th century, Kop was adapted by Polish players in regions under Prussian influence. It became a distinct Polish identity game through the inclusion of the 'Wedding' (Wesele) contract.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players playing in partnerships or solo depending on the contract.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '16 French-suited cards: Ace, 10, Queen, Jack in four suits. Aces are not the highest! Order: â™¥A > â™£10 > â™ 10 > â™¥10 > Queens > Jacks > â™¢A > â™¢10.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '16 French-suited cards: Ace, 10, Queen, Jack in four suits. Aces are not the highest! Order: â™¥A > â™£10 > â™ 10 > â™¥10 > Queens > Jacks > â™¢A > â™¢10.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer deals 4 cards to each player (usually in a single round).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'As-dycha (classic): Classic order: 12 trumps (Hearts A-10, Q-J, Diamonds A-10), clubs and spades two cards each.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'As-trzy dychy: Black tens promoted above club Queen; 14 trumps, one card each in clubs and spades.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Kontra to own wesele: Some allow a player to say kontra to their own wesele (not to zolo or zolo du).',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
